1
00:00:21,390 --> 00:00:23,060
is nothing more familiar, more breathtaking in its action, marvelous in its mechanics, exquisite in its range of senses,

2
00:00:32,730 --> 00:00:35,400
and staggering in its ability to understand.

3
00:00:38,070 --> 00:00:41,070
On a fantastic voyage through a single day, we plunge deep into

4
00:00:41,410 --> 00:00:45,750
the routine miracles of the human body.

5
00:00:53,420 --> 00:00:53,750
Our instruments, engines, infrastructure, roadways and circuitry, through 10,000

6
00:01:01,430 --> 00:01:02,760
blinks, 20,000 breaths, 100,000 beats.

7
00:01:21,110 --> 00:01:24,120
Today is an ordinary, extraordinary day in the life of the incredible human machine.

8
00:01:28,790 --> 00:01:30,790
The incredible human machine in action.

9
00:01:35,130 --> 00:01:36,460
Just about every body part, from our skin receptors to the balance center in our inner ears,

10
00:01:41,130 --> 00:01:42,470
helped to make these amazing complexities of movement seem almost effortless, automatic even.

11
00:01:56,820 --> 00:01:57,480
But if we peer beneath our skin, we see that one tissue above all propels us into a thousand different positions. From the soles of our feet to the tips of our fingers, some 650 muscles, about 40% of our body mass, power every move we make.

12
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:25,180
As our day pushes on, these skeletal muscles lift us through it, usually without our thinking about it.

13
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:30,520
Without them, we couldn't run or blink, smile or speak for that matter.

14
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:42,530
Hello? Just muttering a single word. Hello? Hello.

15
00:02:53,540 --> 00:02:55,210
Involves muscles in the face, lips, tongue, jaw, larynx, as many as a hundred muscles.

16
00:02:59,210 --> 00:03:00,550
Many of the same muscles let us form a frown, turn them upside down, and all 34 muscles in the face, let us deliver a kiss.

17
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:04,550
Walking, a highly coordinated series of falls that we've taken for granted since we were toddlers, requires no

18
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:31,910
fewer than 200 skeletal muscles. Back muscles to keep you from falling forward. Abdominal muscles to keep you from falling back. It takes 40 or so muscles just to raise one leg and move it forward.

19
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Now adding to that running, swimming, shooting,

20
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,920
riding, and fencing, and you get an idea of how many different muscles are at work

21
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:47,930
inside Eli Bremer, top-seeded US Olympic pentathlete. I think what I do is very normal and someone will

22
00:03:56,940 --> 00:03:57,940
what a typical training day is. I'll tell him, well, I'll swim about four miles and run about 10 miles, you know, it's not that big of a deal.

23
00:03:58,270 --> 00:04:01,280
I mean, I think anybody could do it. And then I get this weird look, like, you must be crazy.

24
00:04:05,950 --> 00:04:08,280
So what propels him?

25
00:04:09,950 --> 00:04:14,290
If we zoom in on one of these muscles in motion,

26
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:19,290
and closer still, at the hundreds of muscle fibers that run its length,

27
00:04:19,630 --> 00:04:21,630
we find two proteins,

28
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:23,960
Actin and Myosin.

29
00:04:24,630 --> 00:04:27,630
And their actions couldn't be simpler.

30
00:04:27,970 --> 00:04:31,310
They link up, squeezing together like cogs on a wheel.

31
00:04:31,970 --> 00:04:33,310
And then they relax, releasing their grip and going back to normal.

32
00:04:39,310 --> 00:04:42,650
If during a fencing bout, Eli wants to strike an opponent,

33
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:45,320
the Actin and Myosin in his tricep bind,

34
00:04:45,990 --> 00:04:48,660
while in his biceps, they release. When he winds up for another blow, the opposite happens.

35
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Now his biceps bind and his triceps release.

36
00:04:59,670 --> 00:05:04,670
Binding and releasing all our skeletal muscles, we get our every motion.

37
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:06,670
And three, two, one, go. The more we work our muscles, the more actin and myosin we make, the bulkier our skeletal muscles become,

38
00:05:21,690 --> 00:05:22,360
as Olympic hopeful Doreen Fullhart demonstrates. Her brain sends electrical impulses down nerves to muscle fibers,

39
00:05:26,690 --> 00:05:40,040
telling the actin and myosin to bind. But for our muscles to work, that signal from the brain must get through.

40
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:47,380
That requires an intricate, but delicate wiring system.

41
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:51,050
Good to see you again. Good to see you again. Thirty-four-year-old Jason Keck severed some nerves in a logging accident three months ago, and he hasn't moved his left arm since.

42
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:22,750
Just a few years ago, doctors could do virtually nothing to fix this kind of damage. Today, Dr. Alan Belzberg and his team at Johns Hopkins School of Medicine will try to get Jason's wiring system working again. The nerve is just an electric cable when all said and done.

43
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:24,420
A very, very sophisticated one, but nonetheless an electric cable.

44
00:06:26,750 --> 00:06:29,760
If we have a nerve that's been broken, that's been stretched and snapped,

45
00:06:32,090 --> 00:06:32,760
we can bring the ends together and fix it.

46
00:06:33,090 --> 00:06:34,760
If we can't get the ends together, we can fill the gap in and splice in some material.

47
00:06:38,430 --> 00:06:40,100
And if we can't do that, then again, we go to this nerve transfer concept of robbing Peter to pay Paul.

48
00:06:45,770 --> 00:06:46,440
Anything? Nothing?

49
00:06:50,440 --> 00:06:53,010
Paul, in this case, is Jason's arm. To find a suitable Peter to rob,

50
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,790
Belzberg zaps various nerves with electricity to see where current is being lost.

51
00:07:00,450 --> 00:07:03,790
Not unlike an electrician checking wires in a house.

52
00:07:10,130 --> 00:07:10,800
What makes this interesting is everybody's wired just a little bit differently.

53
00:07:12,470 --> 00:07:13,470
The hope is that at least some wires are still plugged in.

54
00:07:19,140 --> 00:07:20,140
Sadly, Belzberg discovers this isn't the case with Jason.

55
00:07:30,820 --> 00:07:31,150
All five of the major nerves that leave the spinal cord that go to control the arm and the hand have been ripped right out.

56
00:07:31,490 --> 00:07:43,060
There was no connection with the brain. So far, no one has figured out how to plug nerves back into the spinal cord.

57
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:46,500
The team will need to patch in somewhere else.

58
00:07:46,830 --> 00:07:50,170
This unfortunately looks fairly scarred in here as well.

59
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:53,510
So we say, a somewhat heroic maneuver where we go down,

60
00:07:53,840 --> 00:08:04,090
and we take some of the nerves that normally feed the ribs, normally feed the muscles in between the ribs and help you breathe.

61
00:08:04,190 --> 00:08:05,520
And we're using those nerves now to eventually make his arm move.

62
00:08:15,530 --> 00:08:23,100
Jason's nerves don't reach from his ribs to his arms. So first, they'll have to get extra wiring from his leg. So I'm harvesting the splicing wire right now from his leg that we're going to use. Trading what will become a scar and a numb patch on his foot for a bend at his elbow.

63
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
If the nerves take, his left arm's only connection

64
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,880
to his brain will be through the breathing muscles in his ribs. That means with every breath Jason takes, his arm will bend.

65
00:08:42,890 --> 00:08:45,230
Every breath, that will only last perhaps six months,

66
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:47,900
and then the brain will relearn and stop doing that.

67
00:08:51,230 --> 00:08:54,900
It will take time before we know how much movement Jason will get back.

68
00:08:56,240 --> 00:08:57,240
The fibers extending from the nerve cells that carry electrical signals

69
00:08:57,570 --> 00:08:59,570
grow about an inch a month.

70
00:09:05,250 --> 00:09:06,580
Jason is receiving about nine inches worth.

71
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:09,480
So if all goes well, he'll have some feeling and motion return within the year.

72
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:13,920
So he went through a heck of an operation today.

73
00:09:14,260 --> 00:09:15,590
I'm sure he's going to have a lot of pain from it,

74
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:25,500
a lot of expense from it. But to him, if this works, I suspect it'll be worth it.

75
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:29,940
Nerves to muscles, the system is a marvel to behold.

76
00:09:32,270 --> 00:09:33,940
But whether we're competing at the Olympic level or simply making dinner plans and picking up groceries,

77
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:42,620
the whole thing would be absolutely useless without an infrastructure.

78
00:09:52,630 --> 00:09:53,290
Any muscle to its base, through a bundle of strong, flexible fibers called tendons, down to the very point where it's anchored, and you'll find one of the world's most remarkable materials. Some 206 of these engineering marvels are strewn throughout the body. Strong enough to support up to 20 times our body weight, light enough to defy gravity, however briefly, flexible enough to absorb unfathomable impacts, and connected in such a way as to provide

79
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:01,030
a seemingly endless range of movement. Accounting for some 15% of our body mass, bones are what give us our shape. Without them, we would all look like this. But despite the amazing strength and support our inner frameworks provide, they're usually portrayed in a more sinister manner.

80
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:08,370
Descend 60 feet under the streets of Paris to its catacombs hundreds of years old,

81
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:11,370
and we get the stereotypical image of bone,

82
00:11:11,710 --> 00:11:17,050
dry, sterile, white.

83
00:11:18,710 --> 00:11:19,380
That's not exactly surprising.

84
00:11:22,050 --> 00:11:26,050
This is the only way most of us ever get to see bones.

85
00:11:30,060 --> 00:11:32,730
And these are the only bits of us that are going to be left.

86
00:11:55,420 --> 00:11:56,750
This was the world's first glimpse of bone inside a living human body. The left hand of Frau Bertha Roentgen, as imaged in 1895 by her husband, Wilhelm Roentgen, discoverer of the X-ray. We've come a long way since then.

87
00:11:57,090 --> 00:12:00,760
and it turns out bones are anything but dry.

88
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:06,430
Deep in the center of many bones,

89
00:12:06,760 --> 00:12:08,430
in this tissue called the marrow,

90
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:12,100
some 120 million oxygen-carrying red blood cells

91
00:12:12,770 --> 00:12:17,110
and 7 million infection-fighting white blood cells are born every minute

92
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:21,440
and shipped off to the rest of the body.

93
00:12:21,780 --> 00:12:24,780
Toward the surface, specialised cells continually lay down new bone,

94
00:12:25,450 --> 00:12:28,780
while others do the opposite and whisk away old layers.

95
00:12:33,450 --> 00:12:36,120
This is how bone grows with us,

96
00:12:36,460 --> 00:12:40,460
grows with us throughout our youth and keeps itself strong long after we're grown.

97
00:12:43,470 --> 00:12:45,130
If we suck away all that marrow,

98
00:12:45,470 --> 00:12:48,470
we see bone is mainly a blend of two substances,

99
00:12:48,800 --> 00:12:50,810
the mineral calcium phosphate and the protein collagen.

100
00:12:55,480 --> 00:12:57,150
It's a match made in heaven.

101
00:13:01,150 --> 00:13:03,820
Without flexible collagen, bone would be as brittle as glass.

102
00:13:05,490 --> 00:13:06,820
Without calcium phosphate,

103
00:13:07,160 --> 00:13:11,060
it would be as unstructured as rubber.

104
00:13:11,160 --> 00:13:12,830
Together, they're light enough to maneuver,

105
00:13:13,160 --> 00:13:14,830
strong enough to shelter our most delicate inner organs,

106
00:13:17,830 --> 00:13:19,500
and resilient enough to last a lifetime.

107
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:33,750
Champion gymnasts like Joey Haggerty routinely push the limits of bone.

108
00:13:33,850 --> 00:13:39,750
They train them to grow and adapt to extremes.

109
00:13:39,850 --> 00:13:40,190
Sports physiologist Bill Sands tries to ensure this isn't past the breaking point.

110
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:44,190
Bones are living tissue like all other living tissues. So the function, the things we ask the tissue to do as long as we provide those

111
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
stimuli carefully, slowly, progressively, we can usually get tissues to withstand astonishing things. It is this adaptive property of bone that allows a martial arts expert to punch straight through concrete. You don't do that the first day, of course,

112
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:17,890
and you have to build them up more and more and more over probably multiple years. The same goes for gymnasts whose bones absorb a tremendous amount of shock.

113
00:14:21,900 --> 00:14:23,570
Sands uses a variety of apparatus to measure this.

114
00:14:25,900 --> 00:14:27,570
Like these inserts that measure the forces on Joey's feet,

115
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:29,240
and high-speed cameras to record the impact.

116
00:14:33,580 --> 00:14:42,250
Ready, three, two, one, go. Well, right here, he's over 150 pounds of force.

117
00:14:42,580 --> 00:14:44,250
Trust me, that's a big force.

118
00:14:44,590 --> 00:14:44,920
The forces generally seen in gymnastics are the biggest forces that I think that we've had recorded so far,

119
00:14:51,260 --> 00:14:51,930
in the 14 to 20-ish times body weight.

120
00:14:55,600 --> 00:14:57,600
The unsung heroes of all this movement are not our bones,

121
00:14:57,930 --> 00:14:59,270
but what brings them together — ingenious devices called joints. From our knees to our

122
00:15:07,610 --> 00:15:08,940
knuckles, some 187 separate joints allow our bones to slide back and forth, side to

123
00:15:14,280 --> 00:15:18,290
side, up, down, and round and round like a well-oiled machine. While bones have an

124
00:15:23,630 --> 00:15:26,630
almost miraculous tendency to heal,

125
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:30,300
joints are prone to break down,

126
00:15:31,970 --> 00:15:39,970
whether it's due to football, weightlifting, or biking. 45-year-old Mark Kramer has had eight shoulder operations over the last decade and a half.

127
00:15:44,310 --> 00:15:53,660
Today, Dr. Carl Basamania and his team at the Duke University Medical Center will give him his ninth.

128
00:15:53,990 --> 00:15:55,660
Number one, I hope to be out of pain.

129
00:15:55,990 --> 00:15:59,330
When you live with pain every day,

130
00:15:59,660 --> 00:16:01,000
it makes life challenging.

131
00:16:03,670 --> 00:16:08,340
Basamania first assesses the damage to Mark's shoulder with an arthroscopy camera.

132
00:16:14,680 --> 00:16:18,010
He discovers that one of the tendons that's supposed to hold the ball and socket

133
00:16:21,350 --> 00:16:23,690
joint of Mark's shoulder in place is eroding, causing Mark intense pain.

134
00:16:25,350 --> 00:16:26,360
I can show you on the X-ray, the ball is actually sitting quite a ways forward now

135
00:16:29,020 --> 00:16:31,690
because there's nothing to really hold it in place.

136
00:16:32,030 --> 00:16:36,030
Basamania wants more than to simply pop Mark's shoulder back into place.

137
00:16:36,370 --> 00:16:39,370
He wants to keep it there. To do that, he'll turn to this. A specially engineered biological material called extracellular matrix.

138
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:04,730
or ECM, that has the power to regenerate tissue. Strip away the cells from just about any tissue in the body, and ECM is what will be left.

139
00:17:07,400 --> 00:17:11,730
Mother Nature has done a really good job of bringing this material together. Stephen Badylak of Pittsburgh's McGowan Institute for Regenerative Medicine

140
00:17:18,070 --> 00:17:20,080
helped discover ECM's power,

141
00:17:20,410 --> 00:17:26,420
which, like stem cells, has the potential to heal many tissues in the body.

142
00:17:30,750 --> 00:17:32,420
The real magic of the material, if you want to call it magic,

143
00:17:36,090 --> 00:17:41,330
is that this is loaded with signaling molecules that instruct the surrounding cells and tissues to heal in a specific way.

144
00:17:41,430 --> 00:17:47,100
The best part is that extracellular matrix is found in all animals.

145
00:17:47,440 --> 00:17:53,340
Pig ECM, for example, is not that different from human ECM.

146
00:17:53,440 --> 00:17:57,450
Which is not that much different than a mouse and a dinosaur, as far as that goes.

147
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:01,780
Therefore, when we take ECM from one species and put it in another,

148
00:18:02,780 --> 00:18:06,120
it tends to be accepted as self.

149
00:18:09,120 --> 00:18:09,790
We can even extract the material from one tissue, like pig small intestine,

150
00:18:11,130 --> 00:18:11,790
and put it into another,

151
00:18:12,460 --> 00:18:17,370
like Mark Kramer's shoulder.

152
00:18:17,470 --> 00:18:17,800
We're going to have graft, tendon, and then more graft.

153
00:18:18,470 --> 00:18:29,140
This is what I refer to as a taco repair simply because it looks kind of like a taco.

154
00:18:33,820 --> 00:18:34,820
Basamania simply wraps the material around Mark's damaged tendon and sutures it into place. Mark's own cells will then attach to the graft and within weeks his own body will reconstruct the tissue and heal itself.

155
00:18:45,490 --> 00:18:50,500
For me, this is very satisfying to go from a flimsy material to now he has what looks like a very nice, healthy looking tendon.

156
00:18:51,170 --> 00:18:54,840
He would be back in the gym before too long.

157
00:18:55,170 --> 00:18:58,840
Extracellular matrix has already been used to grow and treat things like the esophagus, skin, bladder,

158
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:04,510
even to heal the damaged dorsal fin of a dolphin.

159
00:19:07,520 --> 00:19:09,180
This idea of coaxing the body to fix itself

160
00:19:09,520 --> 00:19:13,760
may offer a whole new way to treat disease.

161
00:19:14,520 --> 00:19:17,760
Salamanders and newts and starfish can regrow entire legs.

162
00:19:17,860 --> 00:19:18,530
Why can't we do it?

163
00:19:20,860 --> 00:19:23,530
Our goal is to tell the body, you know, we don't want you to just heal this tissue again.

164
00:19:24,530 --> 00:19:25,870
We want you to reconstruct this tissue.

165
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:33,540
Perhaps one day, we will grow a new arm or leg as easily as a wounded starfish does.

166
00:19:44,550 --> 00:19:44,890
And as the sun sets on our day,

167
00:19:45,220 --> 00:19:48,220
just getting two incredible machines to reproduce at all

168
00:19:48,560 --> 00:19:50,890
is challenge enough.

169
00:20:10,580 --> 00:20:12,250
It's a simple biological function,

170
00:20:12,920 --> 00:20:17,250
the swapping of genetic material to improve the adaptability of a species.

171
00:20:19,590 --> 00:20:22,260
But getting together is anything but easy.

172
00:20:25,930 --> 00:20:27,260
The seduction.

173
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
The courtship.

174
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
The commitment.

175
00:20:37,270 --> 00:20:40,280
It's all part of an intricate dance originated millions of years ago and choreographed

176
00:20:40,610 --> 00:20:50,950
over countless generations. So why all the fuss?

177
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:55,960
Why don't humans simply do as an amoeba does?

178
00:20:56,290 --> 00:20:57,290
Just split in two.

179
00:21:03,630 --> 00:21:05,970
If all we wanted was to make exact copies of ourselves,

180
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:09,640
that is exactly what we would do.

181
00:21:12,310 --> 00:21:15,310
But humans, along with practically the entire animal kingdom,

182
00:21:15,640 --> 00:21:19,320
want more out of life than to simply survive.

183
00:21:19,980 --> 00:21:20,980
We're built to thrive.

184
00:21:21,320 --> 00:21:25,990
And mixing up genes through sexual reproduction is the best way to do it.

185
00:21:31,990 --> 00:21:33,000
The dance may begin with an attractive sight, a smell,

186
00:21:33,330 --> 00:21:35,660
a touch.

187
00:21:38,330 --> 00:21:39,670
Soon enough, the heart beats faster, blood pressure rises, breathing accelerates, skin gets flushed,

188
00:21:43,340 --> 00:21:46,010
and whether or not baby making is on the agenda tonight, the baby making machinery is rearing to go.

189
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
Every second of every day, a man produces more than 1,000 sperm.

190
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
That's 60,000 sperm per minute, or 14 million during the course of an evening.

191
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:22,710
They may only be about 2,000ths of an inch long, but 300 million are always ready to fulfill their life's mission to fertilize

192
00:22:23,050 --> 00:22:26,380
one of these.

193
00:22:28,050 --> 00:22:30,050
Unlike a man, a woman is born with her allotment of sex cells, about one million of them.

194
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,730
Only about 400 eggs will ever get released.

195
00:22:39,060 --> 00:22:42,730
Less than half the sperm cells a man produces every second.

196
00:22:46,400 --> 00:22:49,070
Once the egg is pushed out of the ovary each month, it's swooped into the fallopian tube for about a day.

197
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:52,070
Life's window of opportunity.

198
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
If no suitor appears, the egg dissolves.

199
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:08,760
More interesting is what happens when one does.

200
00:23:10,430 --> 00:23:12,760
Of the millions of sperm engaged in this genetic race,

201
00:23:13,430 --> 00:23:17,100
only a few will defy all the odds and glimpse the finish line.

202
00:23:19,770 --> 00:23:21,770
And as the body's smallest cells meet the largest,

203
00:23:22,440 --> 00:23:24,770
only one will make it across.

204
00:23:29,450 --> 00:23:34,120
There was a time just a few centuries ago when some scholars believed that every sperm cell had a fully formed miniature person inside

205
00:23:34,450 --> 00:23:37,120
called a homunculus.

206
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:45,130
Now confidently know, this is not the case.

207
00:23:45,460 --> 00:23:53,470
Deep within each of the body's 100 billion cells is a complete blueprint for a human being, a genome.

208
00:23:55,140 --> 00:23:56,810
Here, our DNA molecules are tightly packaged into bundles called chromosomes.

209
00:23:57,140 --> 00:24:10,490
Each cell has 23 pairs of chromosomes. Sperm and egg cells, though, only have half a blueprint each.

210
00:24:10,820 --> 00:24:13,820
They need each other.

211
00:24:16,160 --> 00:24:17,160
And during that fateful meeting, their DNA fuses,

212
00:24:17,830 --> 00:24:21,500
mingling the mother and the father's genetic traits.

213
00:24:22,170 --> 00:24:26,500
For several days, the fertilized egg journeys through the fallopian tube towards the uterus, growing and dividing along the way.

214
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
About 20 hours after fertilization, it divides into two cells.

215
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:34,180
In 48 hours, 4.

216
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:43,000
12 hours later, it's 8 cells.

217
00:24:41,180 --> 00:24:45,860
And by 72 hours, it's 16.

218
00:24:46,190 --> 00:24:49,530
Each of the new cells is genetically identical to the original.

219
00:24:56,200 --> 00:25:04,870
At this stage, everything from eye and hair color, gender, and even to some extent, height, weight, intelligence, sense of humor, and personality are all predetermined.

220
00:25:05,880 --> 00:25:10,210
Scientists can even

221
00:25:10,550 --> 00:25:14,220
sift through a dish of embryos to choose gender or identify genetic defects and remove them.

222
00:25:17,550 --> 00:25:21,220
But not if couples have a baby the old-fashioned way.

223
00:25:23,230 --> 00:25:24,230
By the end of the first week, the free-floating

224
00:25:27,900 --> 00:25:29,230
ball of cells, now known as a blastocyst, has entered the uterus.

225
00:25:29,900 --> 00:25:34,900
Cells continue to divide here before they find a place to settle.

226
00:25:37,240 --> 00:25:38,240
It's official.

227
00:25:38,570 --> 00:25:41,910
She is pregnant.

228
00:25:42,250 --> 00:25:44,910
As the weeks pass, cells start

229
00:25:45,250 --> 00:25:49,590
to specialize, and forms begin to take shape.

230
00:25:50,250 --> 00:25:50,920
At three weeks,

231
00:25:51,590 --> 00:25:53,590
a groove marks the beginning of a nervous system.

232
00:25:56,590 --> 00:25:56,930
The top of the tube destined to become a brain, and the lower portion, the spinal cord.

233
00:26:02,930 --> 00:26:03,600
By eight weeks,

234
00:26:04,270 --> 00:26:07,270
almost all of the organs and systems in this walnut-sized embryo are in place,

235
00:26:08,940 --> 00:26:14,280
earning it by week nine, the official title, of Fetus.

236
00:26:14,940 --> 00:26:17,280
And now growth really kicks in. By 12 weeks, just as expectant parents get their first glimpse into the watery womb,

237
00:26:27,290 --> 00:26:30,960
the fetus may practice breathing, taking in amniotic fluid instead of air.

238
00:26:34,300 --> 00:26:38,300
Already it can punch and kick.

239
00:26:38,640 --> 00:26:43,310
At 14 weeks, it can swallow and suck its thumb.

240
00:26:43,970 --> 00:26:46,980
By 24, it can hear.

241
00:26:47,310 --> 00:26:50,310
By 32, it responds to music, voices.

242
00:26:50,650 --> 00:26:52,650
Oh, okay, so that's, oh, there are her eyes.

243
00:26:53,320 --> 00:26:54,650
Those eyes, nose, mouth.

244
00:26:56,650 --> 00:26:56,990
And at 40 weeks, give or take,

245
00:26:57,320 --> 00:27:01,990
it's ready. It's ready to enter the world.

246
00:27:04,990 --> 00:27:06,660
That's it. Great, great, hurry. Push, push, push, push.

247
00:27:21,680 --> 00:27:28,690
Oh, my God. Good job. Good job.

248
00:27:31,690 --> 00:27:32,020
She is. She's a tiny girl.

249
00:27:46,040 --> 00:27:47,700
She's a big girl.

250
00:27:49,370 --> 00:27:50,710
In the nine months it takes her to go from

251
00:27:51,370 --> 00:27:54,040
fertilized egg to fully formed human baby,

252
00:27:54,380 --> 00:27:56,710
she will have grown more than 5,000 times bigger.

253
00:28:05,060 --> 00:28:05,390
As women around the globe can attest, getting to this point is no easy task.

254
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:12,400
And yet some 260 humans are born this way every minute.

255
00:28:15,070 --> 00:28:16,400
That's 374,000

256
00:28:19,070 --> 00:28:22,740
every day and more than 136 million every year.

257
00:28:29,410 --> 00:28:34,420
Why evolve such a painful and risky way to keep a species going?

258
00:28:34,750 --> 00:28:39,420
It's the price we pay for our giant brains.

259
00:28:39,760 --> 00:28:42,430
Billions of neurons are buzzing around in that head of hers.

260
00:28:43,090 --> 00:28:47,430
Virtually all the nerve cells her brain will ever need.

261
00:28:48,100 --> 00:28:48,770
Throughout her childhood,

262
00:28:49,430 --> 00:28:50,770
they'll be reaching out to other neurons,

263
00:28:51,430 --> 00:28:54,770
making more connections with each new experience.

264
00:28:57,110 --> 00:29:06,120
With each connection, mind and body fuse to form the thing that is us,

265
00:29:06,450 --> 00:29:10,790
and at the helm, the most remarkable command center in the world.

266
00:29:16,460 --> 00:29:18,800
Every system in the body is complex,

267
00:29:19,130 --> 00:29:20,460
but there is only one presiding over everything we do.

268
00:29:25,470 --> 00:29:27,140
Every second since our day began,

269
00:29:28,470 --> 00:29:30,810
our brains have been guiding, guarding, and giving orders,

270
00:29:31,140 --> 00:29:34,810
defining nothing less than what we are.

271
00:29:39,150 --> 00:29:42,820
Through an information superhighway of nerves fanning throughout our bodies,

272
00:29:43,150 --> 00:29:45,490
our brains keep tabs on every part of us,

273
00:29:45,820 --> 00:29:50,490
eyes and ears, skin and bones, heart and muscles.

274
00:29:53,160 --> 00:29:55,830
A hundred billion specialized cells called neurons,

275
00:29:56,500 --> 00:29:56,830
sending millions of electrical and chemical signals at up to 200 miles per hour,

276
00:29:57,170 --> 00:30:07,180
these three-pound blobs of fat and water let us think, feel,

277
00:30:07,510 --> 00:30:09,510
want, remember, and react.

278
00:30:10,180 --> 00:30:10,850
It really is what makes us human, because it encapsulates everything from our ability

279
00:30:15,190 --> 00:30:16,190
to paint a beautiful picture,

280
00:30:16,520 --> 00:30:21,190
to construct a building, to render violence on somebody else.

281
00:30:21,530 --> 00:30:23,090
If I transplant your heart, for example,

282
00:30:23,530 --> 00:30:30,870
you're still you. But if we do something that damages your brain, the very character, your very personality may change.

283
00:30:31,200 --> 00:30:33,870
And so the brain is you.

284
00:30:34,200 --> 00:30:41,550
And when this unremarkable-looking organ is under assault, everything that defines you is at risk.

285
00:30:46,550 --> 00:30:47,220
They're going to be putting you to sleep now, okay?

286
00:30:47,880 --> 00:30:50,890
I'll see you when you wake up.

287
00:30:51,220 --> 00:30:52,890
Everyone in this UCLA operating room

288
00:30:56,560 --> 00:30:56,890
is acutely aware of the brain's vulnerability.

289
00:30:57,230 --> 00:31:05,570
23-year-old Brandon Carson has a large cancerous tumor near a region of his brain critical for language.

290
00:31:06,570 --> 00:31:09,910
His tumor is very close to Broca's area.

291
00:31:11,910 --> 00:31:13,580
It's probably one of the closer ones I've seen.

292
00:31:15,580 --> 00:31:17,480
The challenge to Dr. Linda Liau and her team of doctors

293
00:31:17,580 --> 00:31:21,590
is to remove the tumor while keeping Brandon's speech intact.

294
00:31:23,250 --> 00:31:24,250
Brandon, wake up.

295
00:31:26,590 --> 00:31:27,920
Which is why once they've opened his skull and revealed his brain,

296
00:31:28,590 --> 00:31:32,260
the doctors do their best to keep Brandon awake.

297
00:31:35,270 --> 00:31:36,270
Brandon, open your eyes.

298
00:31:40,270 --> 00:31:48,950
The brain doesn't have pain receptors, so Brandon doesn't feel anything, even when they do wake him up.

299
00:31:50,280 --> 00:31:50,610
Don't sit up.

300
00:31:54,620 --> 00:31:54,950
Can we do this test real quick? Language is a function we can't map when a patient is asleep.

301
00:32:02,290 --> 00:32:04,960
We can measure muscle twitching and things like that, but we can't really, with 100% certainty,

302
00:32:08,300 --> 00:32:10,630
know exactly where his language areas are unless we wake them up and stimulate those particular areas.

303
00:32:10,970 --> 00:32:12,300
What's this?

304
00:32:12,970 --> 00:32:13,970
Good.

305
00:32:14,300 --> 00:32:15,310
This one?

306
00:32:15,640 --> 00:32:18,310
Using easily identifiable flashcards,

307
00:32:18,640 --> 00:32:21,980
a psychologist tests Brandon's language capacity.

308
00:32:22,310 --> 00:32:27,650
Simultaneously, Liau disrupts tiny areas of his language center with a mild electric shock.

309
00:32:28,990 --> 00:32:30,990
Let's try that same area again. On.

310
00:32:31,320 --> 00:32:35,230
If Brandon has trouble recognizing these simple images while the shock is being delivered,

311
00:32:35,330 --> 00:32:37,330
the team then knows that this is a critical area

312
00:32:38,330 --> 00:32:41,000
and makes sure to work around it.

313
00:32:42,670 --> 00:32:46,000
Jessica, there's an area that I think is safe.

314
00:32:46,340 --> 00:32:48,010
I'm going to start resecting there.

315
00:32:48,340 --> 00:32:51,340
You just keep talking to him.

316
00:32:53,010 --> 00:32:55,680
By imaging the brain before and during surgery,

317
00:32:57,000 --> 00:32:59,000
something she couldn't do just 10 years ago, Liau can cut out the tumor with confidence.

318
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:07,020
And with a living, functioning, awake brain exposed,

319
00:33:07,360 --> 00:33:11,360
neuroscientists get a rare opportunity to peer inside,

320
00:33:11,700 --> 00:33:13,700
down to individual blood vessels

321
00:33:14,370 --> 00:33:17,030
and glimpse the mysterious organ in action.

322
00:33:18,700 --> 00:33:21,040
Mapping the human brain is a holy grail.

323
00:33:21,370 --> 00:33:22,040
We all want to do it.

324
00:33:24,380 --> 00:33:28,050
We want to create a map where we know what structures are where and what the function is in those structures.

325
00:33:28,710 --> 00:33:32,050
But unlike the map of the Earth where there's latitude and longitude and so forth, the human brain is individualized.

326
00:33:34,000 --> 00:33:38,720
Each is different from the other. It's what makes us unique.

327
00:33:40,720 --> 00:33:43,060
At UCLA's Laboratory of Neuroimaging,

328
00:33:45,730 --> 00:33:50,730
Dr. Arthur Toga is just beginning to understand these differences with what is called the Brain Atlas.

329
00:33:51,400 --> 00:33:55,070
It's a collection of thousands and thousands of scans of human subjects.

330
00:33:55,410 --> 00:33:59,740
Various ages, various groups, various genders.

331
00:34:00,080 --> 00:34:02,080
With it, we can watch a brain develop

332
00:34:02,410 --> 00:34:04,080
from five years old to 90

333
00:34:04,750 --> 00:34:08,420
and see it start to degrade during middle age.

334
00:34:11,420 --> 00:34:14,430
Comparing a man and a woman's brain.

335
00:34:15,090 --> 00:34:19,430
His may have more volume, but hers has added surface area.

336
00:34:20,100 --> 00:34:22,770
Here's the brain activity of someone who speaks one language

337
00:34:23,430 --> 00:34:25,440
and someone who speaks two.

338
00:34:25,770 --> 00:34:30,440
A right-hander and a left-hander.

339
00:34:30,770 --> 00:34:34,450
The Brain Atlas looks at our master organ from a

340
00:34:34,780 --> 00:34:37,450
variety of perspectives, right down to individual neurons

341
00:34:39,780 --> 00:34:42,120
through functional magnetic resonance imaging, or fMRI,

342
00:34:42,790 --> 00:34:48,130
we can see the exact spots where conscious thoughts originate as they form,

343
00:34:48,790 --> 00:34:54,800
navigating a map, listening to music, experiencing fear and laughter.

344
00:34:56,470 --> 00:34:56,800
This is remarkable, and you can watch the human brain function in a normal living individual.

345
00:34:57,130 --> 00:35:12,380
fMRI also allows us to watch the brain as it malfunctions.

346
00:35:12,480 --> 00:35:17,150
Two years ago, a severe car crash left 22-year-old Kimberly Lizaraga crippled by pain in her neck and shoulders,

347
00:35:17,490 --> 00:35:19,820
and she feels it to this day.

348
00:35:22,160 --> 00:35:26,160
Just picking up groceries or driving a car can be excruciating.

349
00:35:26,500 --> 00:35:29,500
The pain that I felt was so unexplainable.

350
00:35:29,830 --> 00:35:31,840
I can't even explain the pain.

351
00:35:32,500 --> 00:35:37,070
It affects my job, it affects my whole life tremendously.

352
00:35:37,170 --> 00:35:39,510
Kimberly suffered no brain trauma in the accident

353
00:35:39,840 --> 00:35:42,850
and her injuries are completely healed.

354
00:35:43,180 --> 00:35:53,860
Her pain lives only in her brain. And with the help of Stanford's Dr. Sean Mackey and an fMRI,

355
00:35:54,530 --> 00:35:56,860
Kimberly is learning to control it.

356
00:35:57,190 --> 00:36:05,870
Pain doesn't live in one region. We believe it is ultimately a flow of information,

357
00:36:06,200 --> 00:36:08,210
a flow of neural activity between a multitude of regions within the brain.

358
00:36:08,540 --> 00:36:13,880
What we're working on now is actually trying to control the volume on the faucet, so to speak,

359
00:36:14,210 --> 00:36:16,550
of information flowing back and forth.

360
00:36:17,210 --> 00:36:20,550
Mackey doesn't want to stop that flow completely.

361
00:36:23,550 --> 00:36:24,890
Pain does have its place.

362
00:36:30,230 --> 00:36:30,460
What's wonderful about pain is that it's so terrible.

363
00:36:30,560 --> 00:36:35,230
My son only had to touch a hot stove once to learn not to do it.

364
00:36:35,570 --> 00:36:37,900
The problem is when pain becomes chronic,

365
00:36:38,570 --> 00:36:42,910
when it lasts beyond the time that you'd expect for the tissue to have healed after an injury.

366
00:36:43,240 --> 00:36:44,240
It's persistent.

367
00:36:44,580 --> 00:36:47,810
It robs our soul. It robs of who we are.

368
00:36:47,910 --> 00:36:50,250
All right, Kimberly, we're going to take some anatomical images of your brain.

369
00:36:50,580 --> 00:36:52,580
This is going to take about two minutes.

370
00:36:52,920 --> 00:36:56,920
Which explains why Kimberly submits to the confines of this claustrophobic machine.

371
00:36:57,260 --> 00:37:02,260
While she's in there, the Stanford team gives her a real-time picture

372
00:37:02,930 --> 00:37:05,930
of the activity in her brain's pain areas,

373
00:37:06,600 --> 00:37:07,270
represented by either a line graph or a flame.

374
00:37:13,270 --> 00:37:14,270
By simply thinking about putting those flames out,

375
00:37:14,610 --> 00:37:17,940
Kimberly is for the first time putting her pain out. The idea is to drive the activity down,

376
00:37:26,620 --> 00:37:29,950
and in doing so, we also hope to see that the flame goes down. We still don't understand how the brain does this.

377
00:37:30,290 --> 00:37:32,290
But one thing is clear.

378
00:37:32,960 --> 00:37:35,290
We can physically change the activity in our brains,

379
00:37:35,630 --> 00:37:38,630
and the fMRI lets us watch.

380
00:37:44,300 --> 00:37:49,640
What we showed here is that you can actually focus on a particular region,

381
00:37:49,970 --> 00:37:51,310
a particular area, and learn to control that.

382
00:37:51,980 --> 00:37:54,310
And that's the first time this has been done.

383
00:38:06,660 --> 00:38:06,990
This newfound power over our brains has applications well beyond pain, to mental illnesses like depression, phobias and addiction.

384
00:38:07,330 --> 00:38:11,000
Our brains are highly malleable, plastic, changeable.

385
00:38:11,660 --> 00:38:16,330
We really can control and change our brain.

386
00:38:16,670 --> 00:38:19,000
And so the opportunities are truly limitless

387
00:38:19,670 --> 00:38:23,670
because there's no area of the brain that we can't now tap into

388
00:38:25,010 --> 00:38:27,680
and have somebody learn how to control it.

389
00:38:30,680 --> 00:38:36,350
What's news to neuroscientists has been practiced by others for thousands of years.

390
00:38:37,690 --> 00:38:43,030
Using nothing but simple meditation, some of us have already learned how to control the brain.

391
00:38:53,700 --> 00:38:54,710
Our brains enable us to do extraordinary things, but some are much better than others at harnessing its power.

392
00:39:05,050 --> 00:39:07,390
Buddhist monks have used their brains to dry wet sheets

393
00:39:08,050 --> 00:39:09,050
dry wet sheets on their backs, slow their heart rates,

394
00:39:09,390 --> 00:39:11,060
or be incredibly resistant to pain.

395
00:39:16,730 --> 00:39:17,400
For Souei Sakamoto, a Shingon Buddhist,

396
00:39:23,070 --> 00:39:24,740
it's meditating half an hour a day under a frigid waterfall in Toyama, Japan,

397
00:39:25,070 --> 00:39:30,640
a ritual that dates back hundreds of years.

398
00:39:32,000 --> 00:39:34,000
We have an old saying in Japan.

399
00:39:33,000 --> 00:39:33,750
Clear your mind of all mundane thoughts, and you'll find that even fire is cool.

400
00:39:41,420 --> 00:39:43,760
When you learn to control your own

401
00:39:45,420 --> 00:39:45,660
brain, it may be possible to use the brain to influence the body in very unusual ways.

402
00:39:53,000 --> 00:39:57,770
To understand the brain's power, Richard Davidson of the University of Wisconsin, Madison,

403
00:39:58,100 --> 00:39:59,770
studies the effects of meditation on Buddhist monks,

404
00:40:01,110 --> 00:40:06,440
specifically measuring how their ancient practices physically change their brains.

405
00:40:07,110 --> 00:40:11,120
These are individuals who we can think of as the Olympic athletes of meditation.

406
00:40:14,790 --> 00:40:16,120
They have spent years cultivating certain qualities of mind

407
00:40:16,790 --> 00:40:17,460
and they are virtuosos in many respects.

408
00:40:22,130 --> 00:40:24,800
This is just to keep any water that might drip down from getting on your clothing.

409
00:40:25,800 --> 00:40:28,470
A little bit of water might drip on your

410
00:40:28,800 --> 00:40:31,140
East meets West in Davidson's lab as fMRIs, PET scans,

411
00:40:31,470 --> 00:40:36,470
and electroencephalograms, or EEGs,

412
00:40:36,810 --> 00:40:38,810
which involve this unusual contraption,

413
00:40:39,140 --> 00:40:43,480
are all aimed at monks deep in meditation.

414
00:40:43,820 --> 00:40:46,480
Today it's 84-year-old Geshe Lhundup Sopa,

415
00:40:47,150 --> 00:40:51,160
who's been an ordained Tibetan monk since 1932.

416
00:40:53,830 --> 00:40:54,490
You have an interesting new hat, Geshe-la.

417
00:40:55,490 --> 00:40:58,830
You look very futuristic.

418
00:40:59,160 --> 00:41:03,500
By attaching these 128 wire-laden sponges to Geshe Sopa's head,

419
00:41:03,840 --> 00:41:08,510
the Wisconsin team can record the tiny electrical currents that continuously emanate through his scalp.

420
00:41:11,510 --> 00:41:15,850
A measure of the millions of neurons firing away in his brain at any given moment.

421
00:41:18,520 --> 00:41:19,520
He'll begin with a traditional compassion meditation.

422
00:41:19,850 --> 00:41:20,850
So we'll begin in the neutral state, Geshe-la,

423
00:41:21,520 --> 00:41:25,190
and I'll let you know when to begin the compassion meditation.

424
00:41:27,860 --> 00:41:31,200
Experienced meditators can enter a deep meditative state in less than 20 seconds,

425
00:41:31,860 --> 00:41:35,200
and with the EEG, we can watch as it happens. Compassion, compassion.

426
00:41:40,210 --> 00:41:44,210
Just at the start of the transition, when a practitioner begins to meditate,

427
00:41:44,540 --> 00:41:46,540
there is a very discernible change in these brain signals.

428
00:42:00,890 --> 00:42:02,230
And together with fMRI data, Davidson has located these changes in several areas of the brain, including the prefrontal cortex.

429
00:42:02,890 --> 00:42:07,900
So this is an area here that is in the left prefrontal cortex

430
00:42:08,570 --> 00:42:10,800
that we find more activated in the

431
00:42:16,000 --> 00:42:19,910
Left prefrontal cortex is associated with enthusiasm and happiness, right with negativity and stress.

432
00:42:26,920 --> 00:42:29,590
By meditating, these monks are able to shift their neurons' activity

433
00:42:29,920 --> 00:42:31,260
from right to left. These changes are changes that have not been seen before,

434
00:42:45,600 --> 00:42:46,610
and so this opens up whole vistas of new possibilities that we are only just beginning to scratch the surface with.

435
00:42:47,270 --> 00:42:49,270
Already, Davidson and others have found

436
00:42:53,950 --> 00:42:56,950
that meditation's effects on brain activity can lower levels of stress hormones and boost immune function.

437
00:42:59,950 --> 00:43:01,950
And for as little as two minutes, three times a day, he believes even

438
00:43:02,620 --> 00:43:08,290
those on a much lower plane of enlightenment can reap these benefits too.

439
00:43:10,960 --> 00:43:18,300
Whether we control them or they control us,

440
00:43:18,640 --> 00:43:22,310
our brains, more than anything else, set us apart from all other species

441
00:43:22,640 --> 00:43:26,310
and one another.

442
00:43:26,650 --> 00:43:31,320
But thought, feeling and selfhood are fragile.

443
00:43:32,980 --> 00:43:34,990
Though it's only about 2% of our body weight,

444
00:43:35,650 --> 00:43:39,320
the brain exhausts 20% of our oxygen.

445
00:43:39,660 --> 00:43:41,990
If the brain is without oxygen for just 10 seconds,

446
00:43:42,660 --> 00:43:44,000
we lose consciousness.

447
00:43:44,330 --> 00:43:48,000
Four minutes, and the damage can be permanent.

448
00:43:50,000 --> 00:43:51,670
And unlike other cells in the body,

449
00:43:52,000 --> 00:43:53,340
where neurons get badly damaged,

450
00:43:54,010 --> 00:43:55,340
they cannot be replaced.

